<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Fontainebleau</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Fontainebleau is a town south-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, surrounded by a large forest and known for its royal palace, rebuilt by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-i">Francis I</a> in the sixteenth century and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-pius-vii">Pope Pius VII</a> there from 1812 to 1814, extracting the short-lived Concordat of Fontainebleau in 1813, and he signed his first abdication in the palace in 1814.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/fontainebleau.jpg" alt="Fontainebleau" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nyyynon · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:L%27%C3%A9tang_aux_carpes_vue_du_ciel.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
